grammar: ./parser.mly

This tool tunes one thing: the grammar's %on_error_reduce list. For
each construct on that list, when the parse fails just after the
construct is complete, its error message reads "Assuming that the
<construct> is complete, expecting ..." instead of listing everything
the construct itself could have continued with. Adding a construct to
the list trades a plain "Expecting ..." list for that "Assuming ..."
phrasing; removing one does the reverse.

on the list now (3): block expr list(stmt)

also annotated, but ignored by menhir (%inline symbols, run `stele tune dead`): plus

### ADVISOR: should any single construct join or leave the list?
Trying 3 removal(s) and 2 addition(s), one at a time. Each is scored only
by how it changes the generated messages; the scores do not add up, so
apply one, re-run, and see what opens up next.

  summary: improving=0 relocates-overridden=0 mixed=0 harmful=1 dead/no-op=4 neutral=0 skipped=0
  what each group means, and what to do with it:
    improving             some message gets better, none gets worse; consider applying
                          (after naming any jargon it lists)
    relocates-overridden  its only gains are on states you already hand-wrote
                          a message for; usually skip (details below)
    mixed                 helps one way and hurts another; read the wording
                          changes and judge (details below)
    harmful               some message gets worse; do not apply
    dead/no-op            no message changes at all: an annotation on the list
                          can be dropped, one not on it is not worth adding
    neutral               messages reshuffle with no net gain or loss; usually skip
    skipped               menhir rejected the grammar with this move; not applicable

  No single move improves the messages outright (the usual result once
  the list is already well-tuned).

